From 9222791d63cd6f804393a75350d0cceac8c8a47e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 19:36:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: time the first frame that waits after frames
 ahead from the flip before it

When native Dolby Vision frames stop being presented a frame ahead, the
first frame presented the way that waits also waits for the flip of the
frame before it, which was committed without waiting: the queue of flips
empties. Its presentation takes up to two refreshes by that alone, and it
counted as late and made a gap of 83 ms between shown frames at 23.976 Hz
in the statistics, at every such change: with the presentation a frame
ahead switched on and off 11 times in 110 s on an integrated Radeon, the
log showed late presentations and gaps of 83 ms that were not missed
refreshes. Now whether it came with its refresh, and the gap, are taken
from the flip of the frame before (the time of its out-fence, the kernel's
CLOCK_MONOTONIC, which is steady_clock's on Linux; taken only when it is a
moment before now and not long before) to its own. The stall check still
takes the time the presentation took.

A model of the presentation (ahead_model.py: PresentRender()'s two ways,
the latency adjustment, and PrepareNextRender()'s choice of the frame) of
720 cases of frame phase and CPU and GPU times, switched on after 100
frames and off after 220: where a frame drawn the way that waits keeps up
by itself (580), no frame is shown twice or skipped at either switch, and
none in 200 cases with GPU times of 22 to 30 ms; without the adjustment of
the latency, 20 of 20 phases show one (the model sees it). The first frame
that waits after frames ahead took 69 to 83 ms to present in all of them,
over 1.5 refreshes, and 41.7 ms from the flip before it to its own.

Not verified: the times on a GPU and a display (the model is of the code as
read, not of a driver).
---
 .../windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp | 31 +++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 29 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp b/xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp
index 4d48b0c..d8bd0ad 100644
--- a/xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp
@@ -355,6 +355,9 @@ void CWinSystemGbmGLESContext::PresentRender(bool rendered, bool videoLayer)
     // output carries the transport already, and Kodi has asked for nothing else since
     bool plainFrame = false;
     bool copied = false; //!< Hades: the check copied the transport of this frame
+    //! Hades: the time of the flip of the frame before, a frame ahead, that this frame presented the
+    //! way that waits waited for (0: none)
+    int64_t drainFlipNs = 0;
     // Hades: with something drawn over the picture, the level 5 block that the display is sent
     // is taken out to it (CDVBridgeGLES::Pack()): beyond which edges is measured where the CPU
     // waits for the GPU anyway (the way that waits, below); a frame ahead it does not, and every
@@ -434,7 +437,11 @@ void CWinSystemGbmGLESContext::PresentRender(bool rendered, bool videoLayer)
             flipBefore >= 0 && WaitFence(flipBefore, DV_FLIP_WAIT_MS, &flippedNs))
         {
           if (m_dvAheadPending)
+          {
             DVAheadFlipped(flippedNs);
+            if (!fenced)
+              drainFlipNs = flippedNs;
+          }
           m_dvAheadPending = false;
         }
       }
@@ -501,7 +508,27 @@ void CWinSystemGbmGLESContext::PresentRender(bool rendered, bool videoLayer)
       m_dvStats.presentSumMs += presentMs;
       m_dvStats.presentMaxMs = std::max(m_dvStats.presentMaxMs, presentMs);
       const double periodMs = DVRefreshPeriodMs();
-      const bool late = periodMs > 0 && presentMs > 1.5 * periodMs;
+      // Hades: the first frame presented the way that waits after frames presented a frame ahead
+      // also waited for the flip of the one before it (the queue of flips empties): its
+      // presentation takes up to two refreshes by that alone, and it counted as late and as a gap of
+      // 83 ms at 23.976 Hz at every such change. Whether it came with its refresh is from that flip
+      // to its own, and so is the gap between them. (The kernel's time of the flip is of
+      // CLOCK_MONOTONIC, steady_clock's on Linux; taken only when it is a moment before now, and not
+      // long before.)
+      double lateMs = presentMs;
+      auto shownBefore = m_dvStats.lastShown;
+      if (drainFlipNs > 0)
+      {
+        const auto flipped = std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point(
+            std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::steady_clock::duration>(
+                std::chrono::nanoseconds(drainFlipNs)));
+        if (flipped <= now && now - flipped < std::chrono::seconds(1))
+        {
+          lateMs = std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(now - flipped).count();
+          shownBefore = flipped;
+        }
+      }
+      const bool late = periodMs > 0 && lateMs > 1.5 * periodMs;
       if (late)
         m_dvStats.late++;
       // Hades: a frame that was taken and whose flip did not come; the output ends when they do
@@ -523,7 +550,7 @@ void CWinSystemGbmGLESContext::PresentRender(bool rendered, bool videoLayer)
         if (m_dvStats.hasLastShown)
           m_dvStats.gapMaxMs = std::max(
               m_dvStats.gapMaxMs,
-              std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(now - m_dvStats.lastShown).count());
+              std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(now - shownBefore).count());
         m_dvStats.lastShown = now;
         m_dvStats.hasLastShown = true;
         m_dvStats.shown++;
-- 
2.53.0

